From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH 45/45] cuda/hip: co-launch the GDN F32 matvec group with the
 qkv+z grouped mmvq (+1.3% decode, BYTE-EXACT)

Round 30 measured where the decode token actually goes on this box, and the
answer reframes the remaining headroom. The CPU is not in the loop at all:
over 50 steady decode tokens the period is 6.715 ms and 6.655 ms of it is
spent inside hipStreamSynchronize, leaving 46 us/token (0.7%) outside any HIP
call. So the ~1.0 ms/token that is not kernel time is GPU-side graph-replay
cost. A standalone probe puts a serial HIP-graph node at 2.25-2.54 us on
gfx1201 for a trivial kernel; fifteen ROCm env knobs move it not at all
(HIP_FORCE_DEV_KERNARG and AMD_DIRECT_DISPATCH are 25% WORSE); and capturing
two independent chains onto a second stream costs 12.8 us/node - 5x worse
than the serialization it removes, which closes the multi-stream idea for
good. That leaves merging launches.

Every GDN layer emits the tiny F32 alpha/beta matvec group immediately BEFORE
the 35 us qkv+z grouped mmvq, and the two are independent: same activation,
disjoint destinations, nothing but views and the F32 group's own folded
epilogue in between. This patch appends the F32 rows as extra blocks of the
quantized launch:

- mmvq_grouped_args carries an F32 co-launch head (weights, destinations,
  bias/scale, postop, segment table, its own ncols/stride).
- The grouped mmvq kernel runs the mul_mat_vec_f_grouped body verbatim for
  blocks [0, f_nrows): same one-warp-per-row shape, same lane->column
  mapping (col = threadIdx.x + n*warp_size, ascending), same two
  ggml_cuda_mad per float2, same warp_reduce_sum, same sigmoid / softplus
  epilogue expressions. Bit-identical to the standalone launch.
- Detection runs at the mmvf group's own site and pulls the quantized group
  down into it. The F32 group comes FIRST in node order, so the opposite
  direction does not exist in this graph - a forward-scanning first version
  fired exactly zero times.

Two things decide whether this works at all.

1. BLOCK ORDER. With the F32 blocks appended at the END of the grid they are
   scheduled last, each occupies 1 of the block's 8 warps, and they extend the
   kernel's tail by their full latency: that arrangement measures -1.50%
   decode (5/5 rounds separated) even though it removes the same 30 dispatches
   per token. At the FRONT they retire inside the quantized group's memory
   time and the same 30 removed dispatches become +1.30%. When merging two
   kernels of very different sizes on this part, put the small one first.

2. THE TWO MERGED GROUPS MUST BE CHECKED AGAINST EACH OTHER. Hoist legality
   walks the nodes BETWEEN the two sites and deliberately excludes the nodes
   the co-launch itself computes, so it says nothing about the two things
   being merged. As separate launches they were ordered by the stream; inside
   one kernel they are not, so aliasing between the quantized writes and the
   F32 writes/inputs becomes a race. ggml-alloc's layout is shape-dependent,
   so this showed up only at long context: server greedy read 5/6 (the
   1000-token prompt) while -c 512 perplexity, decode-path perplexity and the
   five short prompts were all bit-identical. Running the same arm twice is
   6/6, so that 5/6 was a real difference, not harness noise. The explicit
   cross-product disjointness check declines the co-launch instead of racing.

Measured on the R9700 box (Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_M), interleaved rounds
against a binary built from this patch's own parent commit, with the control's
RUNPATH verified by ldd (it resolves libggml-hip.so.0 into its own snapshot):

  candidate tg128 149.99 149.92 149.71 149.66 149.55  (mean 149.77)
  control   tg128 147.91 147.90 147.89 147.79 147.73  (mean 147.84)
  => +1.30% decode, 5/5 rounds separated (min cand > max ctl)

Re-confirmed after the race fix: candidate 149.40/149.30/149.37 against
control 147.42/147.49/147.45, +1.30%, 3/3 separated. Same-binary toggle
(GGML_CUDA_MMVQ_F32_COLAUNCH=0) agrees: +1.33%.

Prefill is flat and untouched by construction - the grouped mmvq path is
decode-only. Five interleaved pp512 rounds, arm order alternated:
candidate 4956.6/4982.7/4920.0/4933.4/4980.6 (mean 4954.6) against control
4975.1/4920.4/4955.6/4961.1/4954.7 (mean 4953.4), +0.02%.

Firing proven by kernel census diff, not a debug print: 25238 vs 26288
dispatches over 35 tokens, exactly -30.0/token, and mul_mat_vec_f_grouped
drops from 70 to 40 launches per token.

Correctness: decode-path perplexity (-b 512 -ub 1 --chunks 8) is
bit-identical, 8.8713 candidate vs 8.8713 control, and server greedy
(llama-server, 6 prompts, decode shape, every completion >= 276 B) is 6/6
byte-identical against the toggle-off control.

GGML_CUDA_MMVQ_F32_COLAUNCH=0 restores the separate grouped mmvf launch.
